#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124198 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3961" :question/problem "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/483fa8080bd245ed3dd303ce993001d9.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/f7e8678ffa8b2c33f49efd58711651f5.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3962" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/890bcf1d83358c5e4338a6e24ffa2521.png)\n\n1. $[MATH: (-3,-1]]$\n2. $[MATH: (0,4]]$\n3. $[MATH: [-2,2)]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $[MATH: x\\in (-3,-1].]$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/370f99885675d6ad20854a2868c28fde.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $[MATH: (0,4]]$ and $[MATH: [-2,2)]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/eb2025e80374eec32f3743673ee7342c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/id #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3963" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $[MATH: x \\in (-\\infty, 2]]$\n2. $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n3. $[MATH: x \\in (-1,4]]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2\n$$\n\ncan be obtained in the following way:\n\n1. Take the curve $y= \\mid x \\mid$.\n2. Translate it by $2$ units to the right, to get $y= \\mid x-2 \\mid$.\n3. Finally, translate $y= \\mid x-2 \\mid$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/d0d8a9c6b3405531dde3d55701c507e1.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $[MATH: x\\in(-\\infty,2].]$ Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/bd8e8f0254d6b5268fc5a78ef6f9bbb4.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $[MATH: x \\in (-1,4].]$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/2c1748b44205856a5d6b2aa9d14612ba.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af504aab-6ea2-5d7d-b0f5-af311f7335e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244188} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf69edd-0c09-5d85-9881-62e41383942f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244189} {:answer/id #uuid "5904e036-d0b5-526a-a30e-2e0b1236bb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244190} {:answer/id #uuid "f78e73fd-fe1d-54a2-aeee-f59b8c88015d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244191} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b80c12-cc51-5630-bd55-cc88cdfdb4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5b80c12-cc51-5630-bd55-cc88cdfdb4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244192} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a2b76bd-f981-5827-b6df-395eb58120bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244187}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "738dd895-faa9-59f9-902b-04ed647fcfd6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-108581" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/89b0be0ca7ae1a6e76d32bfc669d6f554deaaad2055b2875e3f6a49821043205.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/cfb095836b7498c91a5cbd9d2cb694715a0d89a12a5e6ea5ff417342c4bde798.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ce3887-e780-5a2e-98dc-72cb17c19e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244195} {:answer/id #uuid "1544d6c2-514b-5ed3-a321-02a55ec8d2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244196} {:answer/id #uuid "bab0805a-3bdc-5759-8db9-8e433188df0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244197} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ba4da6-7589-5edd-a247-29ec893f8378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244198} {:answer/id #uuid "abd9ffe3-02a4-5525-b139-75722c29e13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ce3887-e780-5a2e-98dc-72cb17c19e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244195} :answer-field/id #uuid "63063725-9c06-5e40-9a60-3fcd720089ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e08394b-d0b3-5e84-8365-84dd1770cf90" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-108673" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/3db32ea943ff1f457704a29f325a592eb4e1e0e53ee258041d08346bf9da4716.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,0]$\n- $x\\in (-3,0)$\n- $x\\in (0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,0]$ and $x\\in (0,2).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/46e1daf2cb9025b6a736f223fbe168ea32b66302cebea23ca3a2d473e78b77b3.png)\n\n        \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $(-3,0)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/d2ed3670f7c67d60c7739ee35d66943b8341b2a49faa97546242609dd39afabc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00bf83c4-61fd-509d-b03a-3081edca0abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244202} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3ccbba-7692-5b9f-8f6a-d2f872405d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244203} {:answer/id #uuid "30f273d6-04c3-5a35-bff9-f86644a94b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244204} {:answer/id #uuid "ce204ac1-108e-5ada-abc7-89ed9f70b592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244205} {:answer/id #uuid "91093f4e-b466-5a4d-a6b5-f81025a64891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b3ccbba-7692-5b9f-8f6a-d2f872405d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244203} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e52a2a-b6fb-5231-b075-aecb8b2b042a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244201}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdd9b8a8-3521-5250-8471-b1939a55f628" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-36002" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/c51c5a4de562e5a3301ea76afc6af57b459acd4c46e5c65d54afc226d883a137.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,0]$\n- $x\\in (-3,1)$\n- $x\\in [0,3)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,0]$ and $x\\in [0,3).$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/00a80dde24ca012e0282362224c8b63764f5e149a8bfcda9c76ff4eb958fa93a.png)\n\n \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/9ea54918e2ef4310b58e9cf8a0c9b1f9757a404d97dbbfd9e5b037af2193f38c.png)\n\n \n\nHence, the correct answer is 'I and III only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f99dde-2516-51af-841e-f3278edc61b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244209} {:answer/id #uuid "78205b08-181d-5dcb-be41-5cacf40a0ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244210} {:answer/id #uuid "84998050-7b40-5f16-9063-fcebbe0cb995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244211} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4637d-ce78-58d7-9bbd-3dc310d0458b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244212} {:answer/id #uuid "b484bb7a-4b44-52fd-9b5f-e13a70a4dc4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f99dde-2516-51af-841e-f3278edc61b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244209} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd3ca4b-2179-5da1-b39e-05542e389c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244208}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1576eb0-9e79-5e65-81b7-76d09de9784a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-36004" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in [-6,0]$\n- $x\\in [-6,4)$\n- $x\\in [4,10)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3f621d-87a9-55d3-955b-17f9e012a4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244216} {:answer/id #uuid "07501a66-868d-58d0-b9f4-9596bc272343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244217} {:answer/id #uuid "13f995c5-1ae6-5459-93cb-1c91845c2866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244218} {:answer/id #uuid "3119ef8c-12a5-5da8-a5aa-f57ee2250014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244219} {:answer/id #uuid "124bcde7-2790-5932-8fe1-f5a3e35f4eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f995c5-1ae6-5459-93cb-1c91845c2866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244218} :answer-field/id #uuid "92d8ecc6-9335-5095-9a7d-fdf6881b1c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244215}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cd22e19-bd25-51c4-940d-5221f04a8731" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-36061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/4d4353286b5d1668d2a67539db8b1fd27227872b1f5a2eaa5fd6015716f255b0.png)\n\nDetermine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-3,4).$\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in [1,4).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/a709558673202ef4a7835dcea6e23b7ec5b13684272c56023058223c01fb8061.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in [-3,1].$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/5a65e71f571e9510580ba5fe105a6eac48acfe4564c5a6bf76371c2bf09ff651.png)\n\nTherefore, the largest interval on which the function is invertible is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc79d0f-f470-5b01-8966-5d49618570c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244223} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76b358-2b74-52da-b5a1-414c8d452f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244224} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd16930-a43a-5142-b4db-22083c4791a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244225} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a64aee-d214-52af-8221-3e9095157c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244226} {:answer/id #uuid "17b451d9-51a7-5a20-ab99-f3e2f6f46644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b451d9-51a7-5a20-ab99-f3e2f6f46644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227} :answer-field/id #uuid "0479c94b-3a90-5efc-9f60-f99d81a78b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244222}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a35aff9-b532-5679-a853-22b41fc9f74f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-36063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/1dd593e8b48b13ec512a3e0c823ec0aaac4f5341bd519c7bb9b04648f95f4251.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/2a68cbeceea9173ab7a53d334d073e9779b048ad8946fd3ff81b09adb79cee98.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7d0ca0-7602-5974-a24b-7a49bdb1f021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122645} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cbe9ce-9211-518e-802c-f018c55edb67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122646} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0bfaea-2fe0-5d38-a426-45d2edb63aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122647} {:answer/id #uuid "228ec388-ee04-5b76-b68a-f72bf5ac82e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122648} {:answer/id #uuid "4b291071-a48d-5362-a424-8c66a69304af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0bfaea-2fe0-5d38-a426-45d2edb63aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122647} :answer-field/id #uuid "704436de-305b-5016-bafe-eed85ac8ef78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122644}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-36941" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/q-36941.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n1. $[MATH: x ∈ (-2, 0]]$\n2. $[MATH: x ∈ (-2, 2]]$\n3. $x ∈ [- 4,-2]∪[2, 4]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8711068c-32aa-5adf-8e3c-3b5bddb4a75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123689} {:answer/id #uuid "accbf09d-4f3d-5da6-bd81-df5467620a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123690} {:answer/id #uuid "f12a250b-237c-536a-8e99-cddd0504367f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123691} {:answer/id #uuid "5374cfe8-90ce-5552-bcb3-d52e0c0c6a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123692} {:answer/id #uuid "7c060e43-578e-5d7e-a4e7-bc12db9ea0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "accbf09d-4f3d-5da6-bd81-df5467620a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123690} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae902fc9-f4cf-51e2-a585-9915ff735075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123688}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ea11e93-5fca-5cd0-afe6-c972b9a2cce3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66482" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^{2} + 2$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x ∈ (-2, 2)$\n2. $[MATH: x ∈ [0, ∞)]$\n3. $[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 0]]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units up to get $f(x)={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/ff3d1ac7500d83574b8671b23e99d4a0f1dbd6ab9cebc906aa06ff2c21f95345.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/a71ce036adc5dc0b15fce9e367a0c8512699dc6a7814f4e9a1886696d2abad6e.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and  $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/6858bd1fab8d9659c84fa941cd63ee9f509c8364dce0e753706c640ecfe6e9ec.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a878299f-3d5d-5d5b-a2ad-690c433fbf64" :answer/ty