we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that  the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e105dc6a-b2b7-5d06-991a-9aa232897341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244237} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2ffd3c-4133-56e6-94f7-288adc04f922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244238} {:answer/id #uuid "dc530130-8ef2-5009-ba06-7b4ea32e851e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244239} {:answer/id #uuid "4387d580-15ab-53d8-bf47-9bd0c712db9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244240} {:answer/id #uuid "eb49808a-5ab6-5a8b-8473-b2ba23c40ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e105dc6a-b2b7-5d06-991a-9aa232897341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244237} :answer-field/id #uuid "12279b64-14c7-5354-9126-ec3e8e1978ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244236}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f43bc0c4-8972-5c88-a856-a4191c5281eb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66510" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/3897b7fcf079b2522cbd0138631a894cbca1005cd241524cf9f3995965278612.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/95b1bd18e41cba7d6d1175f66be053ffea52a32fbb35e2ad1d126dbeb2d73baf.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and III, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I and III show one-to-one functions. This means that these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca64a04-a47b-5805-bddd-c3572751ccb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244244} {:answer/id #uuid "b725bcb7-48c3-5a89-a727-bba23109152a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244245} {:answer/id #uuid "2293c12a-9691-5dcf-8ad1-908149ea758b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244246} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4035ce-18b3-5f39-aa32-182e76f979f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244247} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f4dce8-7d34-5d0a-a308-f9b3b6b7b327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d4035ce-18b3-5f39-aa32-182e76f979f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244247} :answer-field/id #uuid "9158c3dc-ecb6-5d5a-ae97-eb446d617582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244243}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e687e18c-9edd-57ea-b75b-3cb3764012f6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66514" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/f65062d48189401ba22b1b08637d187eed532d5f93dca7663c2e80743510d5d7.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/cc31f6f291058c7890a9210d5ebfac21cdd961c74c3db5e080d7fca334102b53.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graph III, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f299779-9645-5fec-9d18-5081087bfaf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244251} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebe1cc4-44ed-568a-8cfc-08b9188b8187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244252} {:answer/id #uuid "7a35e99c-1b0e-5efc-bb12-73dbbc9be9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244253} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd370f6-3c93-5eda-9eac-539bb3a27499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244254} {:answer/id #uuid "1e600908-bcb8-5927-9a9e-62b963ec33b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ebe1cc4-44ed-568a-8cfc-08b9188b8187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244252} :answer-field/id #uuid "115f5585-6883-54bd-9a38-1df84dfaba64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d503350-373b-552d-8a35-6045ac022068" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66669" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,5)$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9381b3ca-5691-53f3-8d89-9515548e602e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123695} {:answer/id #uuid "2b014d3a-a4c1-5bec-b5f5-d7189536545c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123696} {:answer/id #uuid "2f97f984-ce2e-5e09-a9ff-2dce19d9e561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123697} {:answer/id #uuid "339391da-486c-5270-a19c-7137c1485049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123698} {:answer/id #uuid "3d270576-6c58-5c5d-9719-d161aa5919b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d270576-6c58-5c5d-9719-d161aa5919b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123699} :answer-field/id #uuid "175ed77a-26e6-56ef-93a7-27f91b318f78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c08528cd-5efd-5572-9af4-e4dc31c682da" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66723" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x + 1$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$\n2. $x ∈ (-1, ∞)$\n3. $x ∈ (-10, 5)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/00d6394343d441ee10fa20ee029b8f89b52ff1e6ded8e7fdbe0756118212f1a8.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-10,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f6cf143-cbdc-53bf-8cd1-7055de3b64df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244258} {:answer/id #uuid "eb199e1f-3386-5f7f-a3fd-e27d1aa32736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244259} {:answer/id #uuid "bd213512-1733-556a-b244-e6486893704f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244260} {:answer/id #uuid "f9086544-2c0f-52ec-ad94-5f2f5f708ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244261} {:answer/id #uuid "514b617e-1470-5d45-ba92-53fbff4f153d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f6cf143-cbdc-53bf-8cd1-7055de3b64df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244258} :answer-field/id #uuid "53edd5a3-e1a4-57f0-a8a0-071b51fb43c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd254a1a-f8a4-5f4b-b81d-c5dcb8bdc11d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66725" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-x+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/aaa05d1ab0fd630dacd81723fe00472cb901fb6f73af5f111f44459476191c0f.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78864212-ca2b-5e18-80ef-347c20e8df9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244265} {:answer/id #uuid "1a48302a-dc3c-563b-9090-ce95e47df86b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244266} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8a037c-1c6f-5e11-8357-a6c2f4c47196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244267} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6a1058-9104-57f5-9a7d-3ce1478fca05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244268} {:answer/id #uuid "9f86b073-6dd1-50b1-927f-426838817586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78864212-ca2b-5e18-80ef-347c20e8df9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244265} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8456194-4870-5651-9907-46e558f25a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb981fb-d1de-5cf1-a91e-ec4693f30623" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66778" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x+1|-3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in [1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x+1|-3$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the left,  to get $y=|x+1|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x+1|$ by $3$ units down to get $f(x)=|x+1|-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/3b9f56280089991c7db7652d84a777edd5c5e012921f0d48362f4fc3e4fd2a6e.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$ and $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/fd0b0fe70e009f43e80b0f712b6cfd576deb4c4c0457f61adae04cf7e44f140c.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/582ec1960b053731bf201c84d8c58fd4e1734a2b085cd5549a01aace4b600b4a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}]}
